{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 and Mathlib proof of two lower-bound constructions for the Gallai–van der Waerden numbers GW(r; k, l) of Mao, Ozeki, Robertson and Wang (the least M such that for every n ≥ M, every exact r-colouring of {1, …, n} contains a rainbow k-term or a monochromatic l-term arithmetic progression), for rainbow (m + 1)-term progressions and every m. Let p > l ≥ 2 be a prime with p ≡ 1 (mod m) such that (i) for every i in {0, …, m} the m residues j − i (j ≠ i, 0 ≤ j ≤ m) do not lie in m distinct m-th power classes modulo p, and (ii) no l consecutive integers in [1, p − 1] lie in one class. Theorem 4: colouring x in [1, (l − 1)p] by (x − 1)^((p − 1)/m) mod p (the m-th power class of x − 1, and a new colour 0 on the zero class x ≡ 1 (mod p)) uses exactly m + 1 colours and has no rainbow (m + 1)-term and no monochromatic l-term progression, so GW(m + 1; m + 1, l) > (l − 1)p. Theorem 8: if instead the zero class 1 + kp (0 ≤ k ≤ l − 1) of [1, (l − 1)p + 1] is coloured by s ≥ 2 new colours z(k), where z uses all s colours and has no rainbow (m + 1)-term progression, the colouring uses exactly m + s colours and is good, so GW(m + s; m + 1, l) > (l − 1)p + 1. The formalization proves both theorems for every such m, l, p (and s, z), the resulting bounds for GW (unconditionally for every threshold with the defining property, and for the number itself given that a threshold exists), and five instances with l = 4: GW(4; 4, 4) > 327, GW(5; 5, 4) > 1059, GW(6; 6, 4) > 2253, GW(6; 4, 4) > 328 and GW(8; 5, 4) > 1060.",
